    "slug": "minor-caged-two-more-notes",
    "title": "Two More Notes in the Same Five Windows",
    "summary": "The minor pentatonic is chapter 2's Am triad with a 4 and a b7 added to the same five windows, so three of its five notes are the chord and two are not.",
    "tags": ["caged", "minor"],
    "readingTimeMin": 4,
    "publishedAt": "2026-08-14"
  },
  "blocks": [
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Chapter 2 closed on a promise: "
        },
        {
          "text": "the same five windows carry the next layer too",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "minor-caged-five-shapes-one-neck"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": ", and no new shape is ever asked for. Here's the layer. The minor pentatonic is the triad you already own — "
        },
        {
          "text": "1 b3 5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — with two notes added: the "
        },
        {
          "text": "4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Five degrees to the octave, "
        },
        {
          "text": "1 b3 4 5 b7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", which in A is "
        },
        {
          "text": "A C D E G",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": "."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Here's the trap, and it's worth naming before anything else. Chapter 1 taught that "
        },
        {
          "text": "three notes drop",
          "link": {
            "kind": "article",
            "slug": "minor-caged-the-three-that-drop"
          }
        },
        {
          "text": " when A major becomes A minor: "
        },
        {
          "text": "3→b3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "6→b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "7→b7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Only two of those three live in this layer. The minor pentatonic has the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and no "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " at all. \"The three notes that drop\" is a fact about the whole seven-note scale — reach for it here and you'll go looking for a note that isn't in the window."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "callout",
      "tone": "warning",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The minor pentatonic has no "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". \"The three notes that drop\" describes chapter 1's seven-note scale — it does not hold for this five-note layer."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "What it leaves out: of natural minor's seven degrees, the pentatonic keeps five and drops two — the "
        },
        {
          "text": "2",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b6",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ". Chapter 4 puts both back. Neither gets a position or a character here; they're just the two notes this layer isn't teaching yet."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "live",
      "component": "scale-compare",
      "props": {
        "root": "A",
        "scales": ["minor", "minor-pentatonic"]
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Play both cards. The first is chapter 1's seven-note scale; the second is what's left after two notes come out. Same root, and this time nothing is tinted — the pentatonic doesn't add a note the scale doesn't already have, it only takes two away."
        }
      ]
    },
