    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "The split is the whole point of this chapter, so here it is plainly. Three of these five degrees — "
        },
        {
          "text": "1",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": ", "
        },
        {
          "text": "b3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and "
        },
        {
          "text": "5",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — are the "
        },
        {
          "text": "Am",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " chord chapter 2 spent seven lessons on. Two — the "
        },
        {
          "text": "4",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " — aren't in that chord at all. A player who can tell which dot is which can end a phrase on a note the chord is actually made of; a player who only knows the shape just runs up and down it."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "One thing worth knowing if this looks familiar from the major side: in that pathway's version of this chapter, the pentatonic most guitarists already knew was the minor one, and the lesson had to explain why it wasn't the scale being taught. Here it is the scale being taught. The "
        },
        {
          "text": "b3",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " and the "
        },
        {
          "text": "b7",
          "marks": ["code"]
        },
        {
          "text": " slot into the window you already own, and the five-note shape most players learn first turns out to be the third layer of a system you've been building for two chapters."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "One convention, restated from chapter 2 and then turned around. A window diagram still lights every note of the layer inside the window, not one grip a hand could hold — that part hasn't changed. What's changed is the arithmetic. At the triad layer that meant more dots than a hand could reach at once. At this layer it mostly means the opposite: two dots per string across three or four frets is close to what a hand plays, so for most of the five forms the diagram now reads as the shape. Two of the five still spread wider than that, and their own lessons will say so plainly."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "spans": [
        {
          "text": "Here's the proof, in the window chapter 2 already drew once. The C form's triad, then the same window with the pentatonic filled in:"
        }
      ]
    },
